  <datafield tag="500" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">"Thanks to Lynne A. Isbell. To consider snake detection theory more, begin with her book. "The fruit, the tree, and the serpent: why we see so well" (Cambridge, Harvard University Press, 2009)"--Colophon (snake's tail).</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="500" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">"Rupert the snake was blind-embossed onto Rives lightweight paper by Sara Press. Letterpressed with Denise Figueredo, Figgy Press. Fonts: Honey Badger and Jellyka Delicious Cake"--Colophon (snake's tail.).</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="504" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">Includes 1 bibliographical reference.</subfield>
  </datafield>
  <datafield tag="520" ind1=" " ind2=" ">
    <subfield code="a">"Evolve comes from 'to unroll' in Latin. This book, a snake that unrolls out of a felt egg, considers a recent evolutionary theory ... which proposes that humans and certain other primates developed our excellent vision and intelligence due largely to co-evolution with snakes. A viewer's own visceral response (or lack thereof) to the bookform itself might inform whether one agrees with the theory or not."--Publisher's website.</subfield>
  </datafield>
